----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------DHCP-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP):
	> Client-server protocol that automatically provides a dhcp client (host) with its network configuration details from the dhcp server, instead of requiring manual config:
		1) IP address
		2) Subnet mask
		3) Default gateway
		4) DNS server address
	> DHCP information obtaining process:
		1) Client ----------------DHCP Discover (Broadcast)--------------> Server
		2) Client <---------------DHCP Offer (Uni/Broadcast)-------------- Server
		3) Client ----------------DHCP Request (Broadcast)---------------> Server
		4) Client <----DHCP Ack {Network Config Info} (Uni/Broadcast)----- Server
	> Advantages: centralized, automated, efficient
	> DHCP Client Suitability:
		> Suitable DHCP Clients: PCs <= Work fine even if IP address changes over time <= DHCP server asigns IP addresses dynamically
		> Unsuitable DHCP Clients: Servers, Routers, Switches <= Require static IP addresses

DHCP Server Configuration:
	1) Default Gateway (cisco router) as DHCP Server
		> The default gateway for DHCP clients is configured as their DHCP server
		> DHCP clients send out DHCP requests as broadcasts => Hits default gateway (DHCP server) => DHCP server replies with DHCP info for DHCP clients
	2) Default Gateway (cisco router) supports External DHCP Server
		> The default gateway for DHCP clients is configured to support an external DHCP server on a different subnet
			<= Default gateway is configured to forward broadcast DHCP requests out to corresponding DHCP server
				<= Broadcast DHCP requests by DHCP clients are, by default, not forwarded out by the default gateway
		> On the default gateway, the interface facing DHCP clients is configured with a helper-address (ip address of DHCP (+DNS) server for these DHCP clients)
			=> Any broadcast DHCP requests from DHCP clients on this interface will be forwarded out to their DHCP server at this address

Cisco Router as DHCP Client:
	> For when router is a DHCP client for obtaining a dynamic public IP address from the ISP
		> Could further NAT this to multiple private IP addresses
	> Router is configured as DHCP client on interface level => Interface configured as DHCP client will obtain its IP address from DHCP server



--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------DHCP_LAB---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Config Default Gateway (cisco router) as DHCP Server
	R1(config)#ip dhcp excluded-address 10.0.0.0 10.0.0.10		// specify ip address range to be excluded from assignment by DHCP server
	R1(config)#ip dhcp pool <poolName>				// create a DHCP server with name <poolName>
	R1(config-dhcp)#network <networkId> <subnetMask>		// specify network, clients belonging to this network will be allotted IPs from this network range
	R1(config-dhcp)#default-router <defaultGatewayIP>		// specify default-gateway IP
	R1(config-dhcp)#dns-server <dnsServerIP>			// specify dns-server IP

Verification Default Gateway (cisco router) as DHCP Server
	1. R1#show ip dhcp pool						// show dhcp details for all pools
	2. R1#show ip dhcp binding					// show which IP-address allotted to which MAC-address

Config Default Gateway (cisco router) to support External DHCP Server
	R1(config)#interface f0/1					// interface facing DHCP clients
	R1(config-if)#ip helper-address <DHCPserverAddress>		// config address of DHCP server; requests from these clients will be forwarded at this address

Config Cisso Router as DHCP Client
	R1(config)#interface f0/0					// interface to be configures as DHCP client
	R1(config-if)#ip address dhcp					// obtain ip address through dhcp
	R1(config-if)#no shutdown					// bring interface up

Verification Cisco Router as DHCP Client
	R1#show dhcp lease						// dhcp lease information